# Every value in this tree that is shaped like a credential and is not one, and
# why each one is here.
#
# secret-scan reads this file and nothing else does. An entry is one line
# holding the exact text the check matched. The line immediately above an entry
# is its reason, and secret-scan refuses an entry that has none, so a value
# cannot join this list without somebody writing down what it is.
#
# THE QUESTION EVERY REASON ANSWERS is what the value unlocks. Not whether it
# looks harmless, and not whether it is short: a password nobody chose is still
# a password if something accepts it. An entry belongs here where the value
# unlocks nothing anybody runs, which is an example in prose, an input a test
# hands to a parser, or a fixture another script writes into a throwaway
# repository to show that the refusal works.
#
# THE LIST HOLDS WHAT THE TREE ACTUALLY CARRIES. secret-scan refuses an entry
# that no tracked file answers, so a value cannot be permitted here before
# anything is written in it. That fails closed in the other direction too:
# taking the last use of a value out of the tree reddens the entry that declared
# it, and the repair is to take the entry out in the same change rather than to
# leave a permission nothing needs.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT SCANNED, because it holds the values it declares and a check
# reading it would find every entry inside the entry permitting it. That is what
# makes an entry answer to something outside itself.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT is a judgement that any value below is safe to publish.
# It is a judgement that it is not a credential. If one of them ever becomes one,
# the repair is at the thing that accepts it and not on this line.

# The example connection string, in the guide, in the script that refuses
# without one, and in the workflow that runs the suite needing a database. The
# instance it names is a loopback address on the machine running the suite, and
# the password is the same word as the user and the database.
postgres://kontor:kontor@127.0.0.1:5432

# A doc comment in the database harness naming the parts of the value it wants,
# so a reader knows what to put where. Every word in it is the name of a part.
postgres://user:password@host:port

# The same shape one line further down, where the harness says what it takes out
# of the value before printing it. Shortened there for the width of the line.
postgres://user:pass@host:port

# Input to the parser test that proves the harness prints the address and not
# the credentials. The host is under the domain reserved for examples, so it
# resolves to nothing, and the value is never handed to a database.
postgres://kontor:secret@db.example

# The second input to the same test, on a loopback address, which is what makes
# the pair prove the parser reads its argument rather than returning a constant.
postgres://kontor:secret@127.0.0.1:5433

# The value that same test puts in front of the harness to show the refusal does
# not repeat it back. It is a literal in a test and nothing accepts it.
password = "9tKmNotForALog"

# The private key header prove-secret-scan writes into a throwaway repository to
# show that this shape is refused. It is the header line on its own with no key
# under it, so there is nothing here to be a key.
-----BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----

# The connection string prove-secret-scan writes into a throwaway repository for
# the same reason. Nothing in this project speaks that protocol and no host by
# that name exists.
amqp://carrier:hunter2@queue.example

# The assignment prove-secret-scan writes into a throwaway repository, and the
# neighbour leg beside it writes the same line reading the value from the
# environment instead, which has to pass.
api_key = "Zq4Xr8Tm2Lp6Vd9Kc1Bn"

# The token prove-secret-scan writes into a throwaway repository. It is the
# shape one service issues, made up character by character here, and no account
# anywhere has ever been reachable by it.
AKIAQZ7RTUVWXY234567
